  "id": "urea-cycle",
  "name": "Urea cycle",
  "category": "metabolic",
  "topic": "metabolism",
  "summary": "Hepatocytes convert toxic ammonia into urea for excretion. Two steps occur in mitochondria and three in the cytosol; one nitrogen enters as NH₄⁺ (via carbamoyl phosphate) and the other from aspartate.",
  "evidence": "established",
  "layout": "cycle",
  "location": "Liver: mitochondrial matrix + cytosol",
  "net": "NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻ + Aspartate + 3 ATP + H₂O → Urea + Fumarate + 2 ADP + AMP + 2 Pᵢ + PPᵢ (4 high-energy bonds)",
  "clinical": "Urea cycle disorders (OTC deficiency most common) cause hyperammonaemic encephalopathy. In liver failure, ammonia accumulates (hepatic encephalopathy); lactulose and rifaximin lower gut ammonia production/absorption.",
